<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hokkaido</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hokkaido is the second-largest and northernmost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s main islands. Long known to the Japanese as Ezo, it was the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>, while the Matsumae clan controlled trade from a foothold in the south. Meiji Japan formally annexed the island in 1869, renamed it Hokkaido and settled it with farmers and soldier-colonists, pushing the Ainu to the margins.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hokkaido.jpg" alt="Hokkaido" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:140829_Ichiko_of_Shiretoko_Goko_Lakes_Hokkaido_Japan01s5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Russians and Japanese met at trading posts in northern <b>Hokkaido</b> in 1778–1779, the talks were held in the Ainu language, with Ainu guides translating for both sides.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ainu">Ainu · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Part of this ancestry has been traced to a source population near the Himalayas, reaching Japan by an inland route through southern or central China; another component reached <b>Hokkaido</b> from Siberia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ainu of <b>Hokkaido</b>, Sakhalin and the Kurils traded between Honshu and Northeast Asia until the Tokugawa shogunate gave the Matsumae clan exclusive rights to trade with them.</blockquote><a href="/essays/before-the-state">Before the State</a></li><li><blockquote>It is a sea shanty, said to have been first sung by the fishermen of <b>Hokkaido</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/soran-bushi">Sōran Bushi</a></li><li><blockquote>Ainu culture took recognisable form around the 13th century in <b>Hokkaido</b>, growing out of the earlier Satsumon culture with influences from the Okhotsk culture.</blockquote><a href="/state/ainu-chiefdoms">Ainu chiefdoms</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kakizaki family had established themselves on the southern tip of <b>Hokkaido</b> in the 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/matsumae-domain">Matsumae Domain</a></li></ul></section>
